<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why does the epsilon method give the right answer?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>The roots depend continuously on the coefficients. Perturbing the polynomial slightly so that the zero becomes <span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϵ</span></span></span></span> doesn’t move any root across the imaginary axis (provided none sits on it), so the sign-change count of the perturbed array equals that of the original.</p>
</div></details></div>
